To look up Proof of Concept (PoC) exploit strings for the vulnerabilities flagged by Retire.js, use a methodical three-step investigation workflow. [1] 
Bug bounty triagers on platforms like [YesWeHack](https://www.yeswehack.com/) require a working PoC payload demonstrating impact before approving a payout. [2] 
------------------------------
## Step 1: Query Aggregation Databases
Take the specific CVE identifier flagged in your scanner output (for example, CVE-2020-11022) and query platforms dedicated to mapping exploits: [3] 

* GitHub Search Engine: Developers frequently publish replication repositories. Search GitHub specifically using dork syntax:

"CVE-2020-11022" exploit OR "PoC"

* Sploitus / Exploit-DB: Navigate to [Sploitus](https://sploitus.com/) or the [Offensive Security Exploit-DB Portal](https://www.exploit-db.com/) to find curated scripts.
* AttackerKB: Search the [AttackerKB Database](https://attackerkb.com/) to read analyses by security researchers explaining exactly how a specific vulnerability triggers in production. [2, 4] 

------------------------------
## Step 2: Extract the Functional PoC Payload
For the CVE-2020-11022 DOM XSS vulnerability flagged in your laboratory test environment, researchers discovered that jQuery's old htmlPrefilter method breaks self-closing tags improperly. [5, 6] 
The exact public functional exploit payload string looks like this: [7] 

<div data-image="<svg><p><style><img src=x onerror=alert(document.domain)></style>"></p></svg>"></div>

------------------------------
## Step 3: Map the Target Interaction Vector
To claim a bug bounty, you must find where your target's live web page passes user input into the vulnerable jQuery sink. [8] 

   1. Find Inputs: Search the target website for parameters, query strings (?search=), or hash values (#index) that reflect text directly onto the page. [9] 
   2. Inspect the Sink: Look through the application's native script files to see if those input fields feed directly into jQuery's DOM manipulation sinks:
   
   // Vulnerable implementation examplevar userInput = window.location.hash;
   $("div.content").html(userInput); // Trigger point
   
   [8] 
   3. Inject the String: Inject the HTML payload into that parameter fields via your browser tab. If an alert box pops up containing the target's origin host domain, your exploit vector is validated.

------------------------------
## Step 4: Write the Bug Bounty Report Layout
Structure your vulnerability submission document professionally to prevent triagers from rejecting it as "Informational": [2] 

| Section | Required Content |
|---|---|
| Title | DOM-Based Cross-Site Scripting via Outdated jQuery (CVE-2020-11022) |
| Vulnerable URL | https://target.com[PAYLOAD] |
| Component | Static library asset located at /assets/js/jquery-1.12.4.js |
| Impact | Session hijacking, cookie theft, and arbitrary script execution |
| Steps to Reproduce | 1. Navigate to the URL. 2. Observe the executable alert payload trigger. |
| Remediation | Update library references directly to jQuery v3.5.0 or later. |

